  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Trichoderma citrinum is a filamentous fungus belonging to the Hypocreaceae family, commonly found in soil and decaying plant material throughout New England. This species is known for its role in cellulose decomposition and its potential applications in biotechnology and biocontrol.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Citrine Pigmentation",
      "description": "Produces distinctive yellow to citrine-colored pigments in culture, which gives the species its name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Conidiophore Structure",
      "description": "Exhibits branched conidiophores with flask-shaped phialides that produce chains of small, green conidia",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Rate",
      "description": "Demonstrates rapid growth on standard laboratory media, typically colonizing plates within 3-5 days",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Trichoderma citrinum thrives in moist, nutrient-rich soils and decomposing wood throughout New England, particularly in forest floors and mulched areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Trichoderma citrinum produces cellulase enzymes that are industrially valuable for biofuel production and textile processing, making it a subject of significant biotechnological research.",
